# Strange loops in HMD

This vault is a net of concept cards built from the project's discussions
about strange loops, formal self-reference, and the operations of thinking
that people and AI agents perform. Each card defines one concept, describes
it, and declares how it relates to others. The declared relations are data, so
the net can be queried.

## The five questions

Everything here is in service of the five questions of the project README.

1. [[ai-oriented-language]]: what is an AI-oriented programming language?
2. [[strange-loop-meaning]]: what does "strange loop" mean, after Hofstadter, and for AI work?
3. [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]]: can such a loop be built from executable cards with human feedback?
4. [[ai-support-in-hql]]: what AI support does the query language need?
5. [[learning-the-formalism]]: what is there to learn about thinking with agents?

Which cards declare that they bear on which question:

```hql
graph.edges
| filter(e => match e.data.relation { some(String r) => r == "answers", _ => false })
| group(by = e => e.target)
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {question: g.key, cards: g.items | map(e => e.source) | sort(by = n => n)})
```

## The clusters

**Strange loops.** The term, its defining feature, and what it is confused
with: [[strange-loop]], [[level-crossing]], [[object-and-meta-level]],
[[self-reference]], [[self-similarity]], [[feedback-loop]], [[recursion]],
[[definitions-of-strange-loop]].

**Logic.** The one case that is exact, and a second of a different kind:
[[godel-numbering]], [[diagonalization]], [[diagonal-lemma]],
[[godel-sentence]], [[incompleteness-theorems]], [[fixed-point]],
[[undecidability]], [[reflective-formal-system]], [[reflection-progression]],
[[constructible-universe]], [[mathematical-induction]].

**Computation.** Self-reference in programs, and the limits of shortcuts:
[[quine]], [[metacircular-interpreter]], [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]],
[[computational-irreducibility]].

**Thinking.** The operations and the cycles they form:
[[cognitive-operation]], [[induction]], [[deduction]], [[abduction]],
[[reasoning-cycle]], [[analogy]], [[abstraction]], [[generalization]],
[[summarization]], [[prediction]], [[evaluation]], [[reflection]],
[[revision]], [[typed-transformation]], and the six remaining operations
[[specialization]], [[simplification]], [[generation]], [[decomposition]],
[[synthesis]], [[translation]].

**Agents.** Who or what closes the loop: [[agent]], [[hmd-agent]], [[reflective-agent]],
[[human-in-the-loop]], [[compression-and-prediction]].

**HMD.** The loop as it would be built here: [[hmd-feedback-loop]],
[[observer-card-experiment]], [[render-integrate-execute]].

Cards per cluster:

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.category == "concept")
| group(by = c => match c.metadata.cluster { some(String s) => s, _ => "none" })
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {cluster: g.key, cards: size(g.items)})
```

## Agents

What an agent is, in this project, is the concept [[agent]]. Its
sub-specification [[hmd-agent]] is an agent defined as a card: the text is the
instructions, and the operations it performs are whatever operation cards
exist here.

Agent cards do not live in this knowledge base. They are a vault of their own,
the top-level `agents/` folder, bound to the project by id. Its first card is
the base agent, which performs any cognitive operation. The agent cards that
exist, and the operations available to them:

```hql
fn is_kind(e: Edge, kind: String) : Bool =
    match e.data.relation { some(String r) => r == kind, _ => false }
{
    agents: source("agents").cards | map(c => c.title),
    operations: @[[cognitive-operation]] | downlinks | filter(e => is_kind(e, "instance-of")) | size
}
```

Running an agent is a future design, described in [[perform-operation]].

## The relations

Every relation between two concept cards is one of ten kinds: `is-a`, `instance-of`,
`part-of`, `example-of`, `contrasts`, `distinguished-from`, `generalizes`,
`uses`, `analogous-to`, `answers`. After `uses`, the most frequent kind is
`distinguished-from`, which reflects the discussions: much of their work was
telling apart things that look alike. Agent cards, in their own vault, add an eleventh, `performs`.

```hql
graph.edges
| group(by = e => match e.data.relation { some(String r) => r, _ => "untyped" })
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {relation: g.key, count: size(g.items)})
| Table(columns = ["relation", "count"])
| format(as = Markdown)
```

## Proposed terms

Some cards name things the discussions coined. They are marked, and each says
so in its first paragraph.

```hql
cards
| filter(c => c.metadata.established == false)
| sort(by = c => c.name)
| map(c => {card: c.name, cluster: c.metadata.cluster})
```

## The backlog

Cards link to concepts that have no card yet. Those links are the backlog,
listed with the number of mentions. Names of lens cards are left out, since
those cards exist in the future vault.

```hql
names = cards | map(c => c.name)
lenses = source("future").cards | map(c => c.name)
cards
| flat_map(c => c | uplinks | map(e => e.target))
| filter(t => not contains(names, t) and not contains(lenses, t))
| group(by = t => t)
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {concept: g.key, mentions: size(g.items)})
```

## Future lenses

Five cards describe queries that need AI support the query language does not
have yet. They carry `import future` and are never rendered. They are kept
apart from the knowledge base, in a vault of their own, the top-level
`future/` folder, bound to the project by id. A lens named on a card here is
therefore a name and not a link that resolves.

- [[semantic-neighbours]]: related cards a card does not link yet.
- [[neighbour-summary]]: each neighbour restated from the current card's point of view.
- [[classify-operation]]: a passage labelled with the cognitive operation it performs.
- [[find-level-crossings]]: which link cycles in this vault cross levels.
- [[perform-operation]]: the agent as a language construct that performs any operation card.

The lens cards that exist, read from the future vault without running them:

```hql
source("future").cards | sort(by = c => c.name) | map(c => {lens: c.name, title: c.title})
```

## The whole net

```hql
cards | filter(c => c.metadata.category == "concept") | Graph | mermaid
```
